  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO Office in Brazil is dedicated to significantly advancing labour rights and promoting decent work nationwide over the 2024-2025 biennium.

A key focus is on strengthening the framework for fundamental principles and rights at work and expanding comprehensive programmes to adopt inclusive learning options to meet the challenges of the future of work.

By the end of 2025, Brazil is anticipated to have advanced towards the ratification of some crucial international conventions, including C190, C156, and Protocol 29, demonstrating a clear commitment to aligning national labour laws with global standards and prioritizing worker well-being.

The Ministry of Labour and Employment (MTE) is projected to complete comprehensive reporting on the application of Conventions C100, C169, and C111 by the end of 2025, providing vital insights into their implementation and identifying areas for improvement in labour practices.

Furthermore, efforts are underway to support the development and approval of the Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP), ensuring its alignment with Brazil&apos;s national priorities and international labour standards. In parallel, the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF) is expected to integrate new activities related to international labour standards by 2025, underscoring the importance of labour rights within broader sustainable development initiatives.

This comprehensive approach includes technical cooperation to enhance transparency and accountability in convention implementation, alongside voluntary conciliation efforts aimed at fostering constructive labour relations and peacefully resolving disputes, thereby cultivating a more harmonious working environment across Brazil.</narrative>
  </description>
